  "onboardingPaywallStartTrialButton": "Start free trial",
  "onboardingPaywallSkipButton": "Continue without subscribing",
  "misconceptionHintSubtractionAsAddition": "Looks like you added instead of subtracting. Try calculating the difference between the numbers!",
  "autoHintLabel": "Hint",
  "autoHintAvailableMessage": "See the explanation before answering, since this unit is still being practiced.",
  "consecutiveIncorrectAlertTitle": "Alert for parents/guardians",
  "consecutiveIncorrectAlertMessage": "Your child got several questions in a row wrong on this unit. It might be a good time to help.",
  "explanationSequence": "In a number sequence, the numbers follow a pattern that repeats, like the steps of a staircase. First, compare two neighboring numbers and find out how much it increases (or decreases) from one to the next. Then apply that same difference to the last number in the sequence to find the next one.",
  "explanationShapeTriangle": "A triangle has 3 sides and 3 vertices (corners). The word \"tri\" means three, as in \"tricycle\". You can find triangles in the real world, like in a slice of pizza or the roof of some houses!",
  "explanationShapeSquare": "A square has 4 equal sides and 4 vertices, all forming right angles (90°). A square window or a game board are good everyday examples!",
  "explanationShapePentagon": "A pentagon has 5 sides. \"Penta\" means five, as in \"pentathlon\". Many little houses drawn by children have a pentagon shape: a square with a triangular roof on top!",
  "explanationShapeHexagon": "A hexagon has 6 sides. Think of the cells of a beehive: they have this shape! A soccer ball is also made of hexagons (and pentagons) stitched together.",
  "explanationShapeHeptagon": "A heptagon has 7 sides. \"Hepta\" means seven. Did you know the Brazilian 25-cent coin has a heptagon shape?",
  "explanationShapeOctagon": "An octagon has 8 sides. Think of a STOP sign: it has this shape! It's one of the easiest signs to recognize on the street.",
  "explanationUnitCmToM": "1 meter has 100 centimeters — think of a ruler the size of a meter, divided into 100 small parts. To convert centimeters to meters, divide the number by 100 (because 100 cm fit into 1 m).",
  "explanationUnitMToKm": "1 kilometer has 1000 meters — roughly the distance a person walks in 10 to 15 minutes! To convert meters to kilometers, divide the number by 1000.",
  "explanationUnitGToKg": "1 kilogram has 1000 grams — a regular bag of sugar weighs 1 kg, that is, 1000 g. To convert grams to kilograms, divide the number by 1000.",
  "explanationUnitMlToL": "1 liter has 1000 milliliters — a large soda bottle has 2 liters, that is, 2000 ml. To convert milliliters to liters, divide the number by 1000.",
  "explanationDatasetMax": "The maximum value is the largest number in the data set. Imagine the heights of several classmates: the maximum value is the height of the tallest person. Compare all the numbers and pick the largest.",
  "explanationDatasetMin": "The minimum value is the smallest number in the data set — like finding the lowest temperature of the week on a chart. Compare all the numbers and pick the smallest.",
  "explanationDatasetMode": "The mode is the value that appears most often in the data set, like the most common T-shirt color among classmates. Count how many times each number appears and pick the one that repeats the most.",
  "explanationUnitMToCm": "1 meter has 100 centimeters. To convert meters to centimeters, multiply the number by 100 — for example, 2 meters of rope is 200 centimeters.",
  "explanationUnitKmToM": "1 kilometer has 1000 meters. To convert kilometers to meters, multiply the number by 1000 — for example, a 5 km race is 5000 meters.",
  "explanationUnitKgToG": "1 kilogram has 1000 grams. To convert kilograms to grams, multiply the number by 1000 — for example, a 3 kg watermelon weighs 3000 g.",
  "explanationUnitLToMl": "1 liter has 1000 milliliters. To convert liters to milliliters, multiply the number by 1000 — for example, half a liter of juice is 500 ml.",
  "explanationSequenceDescending": "In this sequence, the numbers decrease following a pattern, like a rocket's countdown before liftoff. Find out how much each number decreases from the previous one and apply that same pattern to find the next one.",
  "explanationDiagonals": "A diagonal is a line that connects two vertices (corners) that are not neighbors — like a cut across the shape. To count the diagonals of a polygon with n sides, use the formula n×(n-3)÷2.",
  "explanationDatasetSum": "The sum is the result of adding up all the numbers in the data set, one by one — like adding up the points from several games to find the season total.",
  "explanationDatasetRange": "The range shows how much the data varies: it's the difference between the largest and smallest value in the set. range = maximum value − minimum value. For example, if ages range from 8 to 12 years, the range is 12−8 = 4.",
